5. BONA FIDE JOURNALISM

5.1 Definition. "Bona Fide Journalism" means the gathering, verification, and reporting of news or matters of public interest by an individual journalist or a news organization for publication to the general public, under recognized journalistic standards of accuracy, verification, and editorial independence, where the Software is used solely as a research tool in the course of that reporting.

5.2 Primary Purpose; Substance Over Form. Use qualifies as Bona Fide Journalism only if the primary purpose of the Use and of the resulting Publication is to inform the public through genuine reporting. Qualification is determined by the substance of the activity, including its funding, audience, distribution, and commercial relationships, and not by the title, format, or label of the Publication, the platform on which it appears, or the self-description of the person or Organization.

5.3 Exclusions. Use is not Bona Fide Journalism, and is Commercial Use, where the Use or the resulting Publication is, or is a pretext for, any of the following: (a) a product or service built on the Software or Output, including a paid threat-intelligence feed, paid research report, paid database, course, training, or paid newsletter or subscription whose value substantially derives from Output; (b) sponsored, advertorial, affiliate, native-advertising, or marketing content, or content prepared for or at the direction of a sponsor, vendor, or advertiser; (c) lead generation or promotion for any product or service, including security, consulting, legal, or forensic services; (d) consulting, security services, incident response, litigation support, or other client work, including a Publication describing such work; (e) content whose primary purpose is to earn revenue from the capabilities of the Software; or (f) the internal, research, marketing, or product work of an Organization that is not primarily a news organization, including a company blog post, vendor research report, or white paper, however labelled; or (g) reporting by an individual who is employed by, compensated by, or holds a material financial interest in an Organization that sells security, forensic, reverse-engineering, legal, or related products or services, or by a publication owned or controlled by such an Organization or its Affiliates, where the reporting concerns matters within the scope of that Organization's business, unless the individual or publication demonstrates editorial independence from that Organization's commercial interests.

5.4 Ordinary News Revenue. Advertising, subscription, membership, syndication, or donation revenue that a news organization or individual journalist earns from its overall publication does not by itself disqualify genuine reporting, and a journalist's ordinary salary or freelance fee for reporting commissioned by a news organization does not by itself disqualify that reporting, provided the Use satisfies Sections 5.1 through 5.3.

5.5 Information Requests. On Written request by the Licensor, You must provide within 15 business days reasonable non-privileged information sufficient to establish that Your Use qualifies as Bona Fide Journalism, such as the identity of the publication, its editorial standards or policies, the general nature of the Use, and any sponsorship, advertising, affiliate, or client relationship connected with the Publication. The Licensor will not request, and You are not required to disclose, confidential sources, unpublished newsgathering material, or information protected by a reporter's privilege or shield law. If You do not provide the requested information when due, the Licensor may treat the Use as not qualifying.

5.6 Abuse. Use that does not satisfy this Section 5 is, and has been from its commencement, unlicensed Commercial Use.

5.7 Attribution. Every Publication arising from Bona Fide Journalism must carry the credit required by Section 6.

6. MANDATORY ATTRIBUTION

6.1 Required Credit. Every Publication must contain the following credit, exactly as written, clearly legible and reasonably prominent in context:

This work used Disrobe, created by 1-3-7: https://github.com/1-3-7/disrobe

Hyperlink styling and line wrapping are permitted if the wording and address remain complete. A generic tools list, site-wide terms, a buried source-code comment, or a link that omits the Licensor's name does not satisfy this Section. This requirement applies to all Use, including Commercial Use under a paid license, and to every Publication You create, commission, or control, including Publications that contain no code from the Software and Publications that have been translated, summarized, reformatted, combined with other work, or produced through an automated agent.

6.2 Placement. Place the credit (a) in research papers, theses, and technical reports, in the methods, tools, or acknowledgments section; (b) in advisories, CVE submissions, and vulnerability or incident disclosures, in the description, analysis, acknowledgments, or references, or, where a controlled field cannot contain it, in a supporting document linked from the submission; (c) in forensic, incident, expert, consulting, and client reports, in the methodology or tools section or a clearly referenced credit note delivered with the report; (d) in court, arbitral, and regulatory materials, in the methodology, declaration, report, or a permitted footnote or exhibit, without altering original evidence; (e) in journalism, blogs, and newsletters, in the article's tools or source credit or a contextual credit note; (f) in slides, talks, video, podcasts, courses, and training, on a relevant slide or on-screen credit and in accompanying notes, and for audio-only works audibly identifying Disrobe and 1-3-7 and directing the audience to the written credit; (g) in product and service documentation and commercial deliverables, in the delivered documentation and its credits or notices; and (h) in standalone excerpts, datasets, and exported findings, in a recipient-visible credit field, metadata, cover note, or attribution file delivered with them.

